19 0 obj
<< /A 48 0 R /Alt (Flowchart detailing a clinical study design starting with data from andrology outpatient records, eligibility screening, selection of 420 adult men, analysis of testosterone axis and glucose-lipid traits, traditional and machine learning statistical analyses, and final integrated interpretation focusing on metabolic risk and testosterone distribution.) /K 0 /P 9 0 R /Pg 36 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
20 0 obj
<< /A 49 0 R /Alt (Heatmap showing partial correlation coefficients between hormone measures \(SHBG, BioT%, BioT, FT%, FT, TT\) and metabolic biomarkers \(glucose, TC, TG, HDL-C, LDL-C\), with stronger positive correlations in red and negative correlations in blue. Color intensity and a side color bar represent correlation strength from minus one to one, with statistical significance noted by asterisks.) /K 0 /P 9 0 R /Pg 38 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
21 0 obj
<< /A 50 0 R /Alt (Heatmap visualizing standardized beta coefficients for relationships between testosterone-related indices \(BioT%, BioT, FT%, FT, TT\) and lipid-glucose parameters \(Glucose, TC, TG, HDL-C, LDL-C\) with significant values in bold and a red-blue color scale where red denotes positive and blue denotes negative associations.) /K 0 /P 9 0 R /Pg 40 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
22 0 obj
<< /A 51 0 R /Alt (Four-panel boxplot graphic shows glucose, total cholesterol \(TC\), triglycerides \(TG\), and high-density lipoprotein cholesterol \(HDL-C\) levels across testosterone \(TT\) quartiles Q1 to Q4. Glucose, TG, and HDL-C show significant trends by quartile with P for trend less than 0.001, while TC shows no significant trend \(P = 0.18\). Individual data points are overlaid on each boxplot.) /K 1 /P 9 0 R /Pg 40 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
23 0 obj
<< /A 52 0 R /Alt (Four box plots comparing the distributions of FT%, BioT%, TG \(mmol/L\), and HDL-C \(mmol/L\) across SHBG quartiles labeled Q1 to Q4. Each plot shows individual data points and statistical significance for trend \(P for trend < 0.001\). FT%, BioT%, and TG decrease, while HDL-C increases across higher SHBG quartiles.) /K 0 /P 9 0 R /Pg 41 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
24 0 obj
<< /A 53 0 R /Alt (Five line graphs present age-adjusted, rank-standardized metabolic traits versus TT \(ng/mL\) for glucose, HDL-C, LDL-C, TC, and TG, each with a blue confidence interval. Glucose, HDL-C, and TG display significant overall P-values below 0.001, while LDL-C and TC show higher P-values. Nonlinear P-values indicate limited nonlinearity for all traits.) /K 0 /P 9 0 R /Pg 43 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
25 0 obj
<< /A 54 0 R /Alt (Forest plot graphic comparing primary models \(blue circles\) and sensitivity models \(open circles\) for glucose, HDL-C, LDL-C, TC, and TG, with age-adjusted standardized beta values on the x-axis and different statistical models on the y-axis.) /K 0 /P 9 0 R /Pg 44 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
26 0 obj
<< /A 55 0 R /Alt (Panel A shows a ROC curve comparing five prediction models with their AUC values: Age only, Age plus TT, Age plus TT and SHBG, LASSO, and XGBoost. Panel B presents a calibration plot with observed versus predicted probabilities for these models, each represented by differently colored lines, relative to the diagonal reference line.) /K 0 /P 9 0 R /Pg 45 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
27 0 obj
<< /A 56 0 R /Alt (Side-by-side data visualizations display SHAP values for feature importance in a model. Panel A is a horizontal bar chart showing SHBG and Age as the most influential variables. Panel B is a dot plot illustrating each feature\220s SHAP value distribution, colored by value magnitude, with red for high and blue for low values. A color scale bar on the right indicates the correspondence between color and value intensity.) /K 1 /P 9 0 R /Pg 45 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
